-- #1047 (S0, CLOSED by Stage A-2 unit A-2.9): two UNRELATED modules each declare
-- `interface Speak x where speak : x -> String` with a DIFFERENT default body, and
-- each is implemented by a METHOD-LESS impl in its own module.  The Core IR's
-- untagged-default registry (`CImplDefault`) carried no interface identity and was
-- looked up by the bare METHOD name, so first-match gave BOTH types module A's
-- body: `A-default|A-default`, exit 0, no diagnostic, on eval AND native AND wasm
-- (which is exactly why diff_compiler_engines never saw it -- all three agreed and
-- all three were wrong).
--
-- ⚠️ The EXPECTED value is derived from the spec, not captured from an engine.
-- DICT-SEMANTICS §5: the interface default is used only when the SELECTED INSTANCE
-- omits the method.  `ifai.DogA` implements the only `Speak` in ITS scope (ifa's),
-- so it selects ifa's default; `ifbi.DogB` likewise selects ifb's.  Under the
-- decided class model a class is `(module, name)` (§8 I4), so neither module can
-- observe the other's default at all.  Correct = `A-default|B-default`.
--
-- ⚠️ The discriminating variable is the METHOD name, not the interface name:
-- renaming the INTERFACE in one pair (keeping `speak`) still reproduced the bug,
-- renaming the METHOD (keeping `Speak`) did not.  A fix keyed on interface name
-- alone would leave this fixture red.
import ifai.{DogA(..), sayA}
import ifbi.{DogB(..), sayB}

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn (sayA DogA ++ "|" ++ sayB DogB)
